1) They start at an early age.  Remember the apocryphal quote "Give me your
children until they're five and they're ours forever."  (Can anyone source
this?)
2) Their programming plugs very well into the basic fears and hopes of the
young, presenting glowing promises of heaven and direct reinforcement 
of good behavior (approval of the social group), and more importantly, 
terrifying images of what happens to people who don't believe.  I've read
passages from a few Catholic "children's" books that are at the gore level
of Stephen King when they describe the flame-torture of Hell.
3) The Bible is so obscure and contradictory that you can counter any
surface-level logical objection (which is all that most people can muster)
with a quote from somewhere, once people grow up enough to timidly question
their conditioning -- if they get that far.
4) The emphasis on regular gathering and group activities brings the 
power of social pressure to all ages -- plus additional indoctrination
("Sunday school") at the most critical ages.

Does this sound a lot like television?  You bet it does.  Successful
cults (government, consumption, Christianity) use similar strategies.

