For readers of  sci.bio.evolution, the apparent bigotry below is not quite
out of the blue, but comes from another thread touching on the propagation
of memes, in which I was expressing my disapproval of (among other things)
the popularity of sports and religion.   I'm a big fan of the
scientist/writers Dawkins and Sagan -- and Gould as well, although I rarely
agree much with him.   The Gould-bashing is becoming tiresome; perhaps this
topic would be more fun.

>jbaker@halcyon.com writes:

>Don't underestimate the old meme-propagation mechanism of high birth rate.
>... Catholicism is fastest growing religion of the last century.

>So why do most offspring of Catholics stay Catholic, or Muslim, or
>you-name-it? Better brand loyalty than cars or cigarettes.

It is amusing to hear the clergy proclaiming that contraception is immoral.
I don't think I've seen this explained before, but consider that celibacy
is actually beneficial to the clergy's genes.  To see this, consider the
following elementary Dawkins-style explanation:

    1. Local co-religionists tend to be genetically related, better than
chance.   Suppose that a priest shares a 10 percent excess correlation of
some alleles with typical members of his flock, as compared with the
general population.  Then this priest has the equivalent of one child each
time he (or she) persuades 11 members of the congregation to have one more
child than they otherwise would.

    2.  In particular, because of this bias toward selfish-gene
reproduction via consanguity, the pope may well have earned that title of
Holy Father.  He may well be the all-time record-holding progenitor, having
already produced the equivalent of  many millions of progeny.   For a
person with such great memetic power, it would be genetic folly to waste
even a moment at individual procreation exercises.  Much more efficient to
spend the time debugging those seductive moral enunciations.

Seriously:-- could it be that some people are more susceptible than others
to religious ideas, and could such susceptibilities have genetic bases?
Before you hit the roof, consider that some people are inclined to
accumulate particular and detailed explanations for particular things,
while others prefer general or "unified" theories at higher levels, even
though these may not account so well for particulars.  This pair of opposed
tendencies was once nicely expressed by Isaiah Berlin:--

"For there exists a great chasm between those, on the one side, who relate
everything to a single central vision, one system more or less
coherent or articulate, in terms of which they understand, think and feel-a
single, universal, organizing principle in terms of which alone all that
they are and say has significance-and, on the other side, those who pursue
many ends, often unrelated and even contradictory, connected, if at all,
only in some de facto way, for some psychological or physiological cause,
related by no moral or aesthetic principle...."

I don't find it hard to imagine ways that this could reflect a small,
genetically influenced difference in some arrangement of
knowledge-representation architecture in a brain.


